### What is a peak element?

A peak element is one that is strictly greater than its neighbors. The array boundaries are treated as negative infinity, which means the first element is a peak if it is greater than the second, and the last element is a peak if it is greater than the second-to-last. A peak is guaranteed to exist in any array where adjacent elements differ.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class PeakElementBasic {

    public static int findPeak(int[] arr) {
        int low = 0, high = arr.length - 1;

        while (low < high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] < arr[mid + 1]) {
                low = mid + 1;  // peak is to the right
            } else {
                high = mid;     // peak is at mid or to the left
            }
        }
        return low;  // low == high, points to a peak
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] volumes = {100, 300, 500, 400, 200, 600, 350};
        int peakIdx = findPeak(volumes);
        System.out.println("Peak at index " + peakIdx + ": " + volumes[peakIdx]);
    }
}
```

```text
Peak at index 2: 500
```

### Why does binary search work on an unsorted array?

Binary search works here because the problem has a slope structure. At every midpoint we can determine the direction of the slope by comparing mid to its right neighbor. If the slope is ascending, a peak must exist further right because the array cannot keep climbing forever (the boundary is negative infinity). If the slope is descending, a peak must be at mid or to its left. This slope-based reasoning lets us halve the search space each step.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SlopeReasoning {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {100, 300, 500, 400, 200, 600, 350};
        //           ↑ascending   ↓descending  ↑asc  ↓desc
        // At mid=3 (400), arr[3]=400 > arr[4]=200 → descending → peak at 3 or left
        // At mid=1 (300), arr[1]=300 < arr[2]=500 → ascending → peak at 2 or right
        System.out.println("Slope at index 3: " + (arr[3] > arr[4] ? "descending" : "ascending"));
        System.out.println("Slope at index 1: " + (arr[1] > arr[2] ? "descending" : "ascending"));
    }
}
```

```text
Slope at index 3: descending
Slope at index 1: ascending
```

### How is the loop different from classic binary search?

Classic binary search uses `while (low <= high)` and searches for an exact value. Peak finding uses `while (low < high)` because we are converging two pointers to a single index rather than matching a value. The loop invariant is that a peak always exists within [low, high]. When `low == high`, that single index is the peak.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class LoopDifference {

    public static int findPeak(int[] arr) {
        int low = 0, high = arr.length - 1;
        int steps = 0;

        while (low < high) {  // strict <, not <=
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            steps++;
            if (arr[mid] < arr[mid + 1]) {
                low = mid + 1;
            } else {
                high = mid;  // mid stays as candidate
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Converged in " + steps + " steps");
        return low;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {100, 300, 500, 700, 600, 400, 200};
        int idx = findPeak(arr);
        System.out.println("Peak at index " + idx + ": " + arr[idx]);
    }
}
```

```text
Converged in 2 steps
Peak at index 3: 700
```

### Why is mid + 1 always safe to read?

Inside the loop, `low < high` guarantees `mid < high`, which means `mid + 1 <= high` and is within bounds. This is a subtle but important correctness detail that candidates often overlook.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class MidPlusOneSafety {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {100, 500, 300};
        int low = 0, high = 2;

        // Step 1: low=0, high=2, mid=1
        // mid+1 = 2 <= high = 2 → safe
        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
        System.out.println("mid=" + mid + ", mid+1=" + (mid + 1) + ", high=" + high);
        System.out.println("arr[mid]=" + arr[mid] + ", arr[mid+1]=" + arr[mid + 1]);

        // After: arr[1]=500 > arr[2]=300 → high=1
        // Now low=0, high=1, mid=0, mid+1=1 → still safe
        high = mid;
        mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
        System.out.println("mid=" + mid + ", mid+1=" + (mid + 1) + ", high=" + high);
    }
}
```

```text
mid=1, mid+1=2, high=2
arr[mid]=500, arr[mid+1]=300
mid=0, mid+1=1, high=1
```

### What are common mistakes?

A common mistake is comparing mid with mid minus one instead of mid plus one, then using `low <= high` which risks accessing index minus one. Another mistake is returning `arr[low]` instead of `low` when the problem asks for the index.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class PeakMistakes {

    // WRONG: using mid-1 can access index -1
    // public static int findPeakBuggy(int[] arr) {
    //     int low = 0, high = arr.length - 1;
    //     while (low <= high) {
    //         int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
    //         if (arr[mid] > arr[mid - 1]) { // IndexOutOfBoundsException when mid=0
    //             low = mid + 1;
    //         } else {
    //             high = mid - 1;
    //         }
    //     }
    //     return high;
    // }

    // CORRECT: compare mid with mid+1, use low < high
    public static int findPeakCorrect(int[] arr) {
        int low = 0, high = arr.length - 1;
        while (low < high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] < arr[mid + 1]) low = mid + 1;
            else high = mid;
        }
        return low;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {500, 300, 100};
        System.out.println("Peak index: " + findPeakCorrect(arr));
    }
}
```

```text
Peak index: 0
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include a single-element array (always a peak), a two-element array, a strictly ascending array (last element is peak), and a strictly descending array (first element is peak).

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class PeakEdgeCases {

    public static int findPeak(int[] arr) {
        int low = 0, high = arr.length - 1;
        while (low < high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] < arr[mid + 1]) low = mid + 1;
            else high = mid;
        }
        return low;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Single: " + findPeak(new int[]{500}));
        System.out.println("Two asc: " + findPeak(new int[]{100, 500}));
        System.out.println("Two desc: " + findPeak(new int[]{500, 100}));
        System.out.println("All ascending: " + findPeak(new int[]{100, 200, 300, 400, 500}));
        System.out.println("All descending: " + findPeak(new int[]{500, 400, 300, 200, 100}));
    }
}
```

```text
Single: 0
Two asc: 1
Two desc: 0
All ascending: 4
All descending: 0
```

### How does this relate to the bitonic array maximum problem?

A bitonic array that strictly increases then strictly decreases is a special case where there is exactly one peak. The peak-finding algorithm works identically on bitonic arrays. The difference is that a general array may have multiple peaks and we return any one of them, while a bitonic array has a unique peak.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class BitonicVsGeneral {

    public static int findPeak(int[] arr) {
        int low = 0, high = arr.length - 1;
        while (low < high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] < arr[mid + 1]) low = mid + 1;
            else high = mid;
        }
        return low;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Bitonic: exactly one peak
        int[] bitonic = {100, 300, 500, 700, 400, 200};
        System.out.println("Bitonic peak: " + findPeak(bitonic));

        // General: multiple peaks, returns any one
        int[] general = {100, 500, 300, 700, 400, 600, 200};
        System.out.println("General peak: " + findPeak(general));
    }
}
```

```text
Bitonic peak: 3
General peak: 3
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should understand the correctness invariant: there is always a peak within the current [low, high] window because at least one boundary of the window points uphill into it. Moving toward the taller neighbor preserves this invariant while halving the window, which is the formal proof of correctness. The only requirement is that adjacent elements differ, so there is always a definite slope direction.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class PeakInvariant {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {100, 300, 200, 500, 400};
        int low = 0, high = arr.length - 1;

        while (low < high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            System.out.println("Window [" + low + "," + high + "] mid=" + mid
                    + " arr[mid]=" + arr[mid] + " arr[mid+1]=" + arr[mid + 1]);
            if (arr[mid] < arr[mid + 1]) {
                System.out.println("  Climbing → peak exists in [" + (mid + 1) + "," + high + "]");
                low = mid + 1;
            } else {
                System.out.println("  Descending → peak exists in [" + low + "," + mid + "]");
                high = mid;
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Peak at index " + low + ": " + arr[low]);
    }
}
```

```text
Window [0,4] mid=2 arr[mid]=200 arr[mid+1]=500
  Climbing → peak exists in [3,4]
Window [3,4] mid=3 arr[mid]=500 arr[mid+1]=400
  Descending → peak exists in [3,3]
Peak at index 3: 500
```
